IO operations:
Keyborad,mouse, IO can read and write into memory without CPU 
using DMA - Direct memory access 
CPU gives access for DMA and handles interruption 
Other times CPU works on application threads 

Non blocking IO application Optimize throughput by :
Thread pooling - fixed size threads 
Reuse thread and reduce performance overhead of creation, shutting down of threads 
No of thread= No of cores 
Above works considering only task involving CPU operation NOT blocking call 

Blocking IO application :
Ex:
News website 
Web applications 
Big data/ML transforming application 


Scenario 1:
Single threaded Web app listens http request and connects to DB to get results 
Consider: Single core 
Thread pool : 1 
Steps :
Parse the user request - non blocking 
Fetch data from DB - Blocking IO call --- Thread idle  
Send response back to user - non blocking 

Above is IO bound application in which most of time waiting / doing IO 
While waiting for IO operation other request will not be served 

Scenario 2:
Multi threaded Web app + Cache listens http request and connects to DB to get results 
Consider: 4 core 
Thread pool : 4
Steps :
Parse the user request - non blocking 
Fetch data from DB - Blocking IO call --- Thread idle  
Send response back to user - non blocking 

Above is IO bound application in which most of time waiting / doing IO 
While waiting for IO operation other request will not be served , all thread may end up waiting for IO bound data 

Thread - per- task Model :
1 IO blocking all = 1sec 
N tasks===> N seconds 

Issue :
Thread are expensive - no of thread limited 
Thread consumen stack memory and other resources 
Too many thread ---> out of memory exp. not able to create thread 
Too few threads used -- lower throughput 

Use Thread pool + ThreadPerTask:
Reuse thread 
Issues: 
Price of context switch ==> CPU busy to run the OS thread scheduler 
                to block and schedule so many threads 
Leads to Threadshing ....
ThreadShing -> CPU spent on OS managing system rather than application 

Inversion of control :
If a process is partly dependent on some externalservice 
 On external service outage , all thread may queue up for access 
   It leads to 100% thread waiting for external service response 
Calls that do not need response will also not be processed 

Thread per task model:
1) Performance issues - contextswitch , resource usage 
2) Stability issues - Inversion of control 

NonBlockingIO 
     - Does not block CPU operation 
     - returns immediately 
To use and extract non blocking IO call response => Callback 
    nonBlockingIO(result->{
        userData(result);
    });

Blocking IO :
public void handleRequest(HttpExchange exchange){
    Request request=parseUserRequest(exchange);
    Response data=readFromDataBase(request);
    sendPageToUser(data,exchange);
}
Non Blocking IO :
public void handleRequest(HttpExchange exchange){
    Request request=parseUserRequest(exchange);
    readFromDataBaseAsync(request, (data)->{
          sendPageToUser(data,exchange);
    });
}

Non blocking IO Pro :
  No blocking on IO calls 
  No context switches 
  callback assist to process data when received 
  Single core enough 

With non blocking IO , Thread per core = Optimal performace 

Cons :

Code readability poor - when multiple non-blocking call are nested 
Call back hell 
   NonBlock1(req1,( res1 )->{
         NonBlock2(res1,(res2)->{
            NonBlock3(res2,(res3)->{
                .........
            })
         })
   });

